<p>This is interesting &#8212; in 1904 the <em>Strand</em> sent a musician to Parliament to record the pitch and rhythm of the speakers&#8217; voices on a musical staff. Some examples:</p>
<p><img decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-buxton.png" alt="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v27%201904/page/140/mode/2up" width="737" height="299" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-74775" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-buxton.png 737w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-buxton-600x243.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-buxton-300x122.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 737px) 100vw, 737px" /></p>
<p>Sydney Buxton &#8220;speaks often and briefly, but into a short space of time he can cram a wonderful lot of words, being one of the most rapid speakers in the House. The dominant note is about C sharp, and the modulation seldom varies in character, the speech being broken up into short phrases, with a downward inflection at the end of each.&#8221;</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-fenwick.png" alt="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v27%201904/page/140/mode/2up" width="739" height="295" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-74776" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-fenwick.png 739w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-fenwick-600x240.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-fenwick-300x120.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 739px) 100vw, 739px" /></p>
<p>Charles Fenwick &#8220;may lay claim to the lowest pitch. His strong, vigorous, ringing style is a good index to the character which has raised its owner from work in the collieries to a seat in Parliament.&#8221;</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-lloyd-george.png" alt="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v27%201904/page/140/mode/2up" width="757" height="315" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-74778" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-lloyd-george.png 757w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-lloyd-george-600x250.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-lloyd-george-300x125.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 757px) 100vw, 757px" /></p>
<p>David Lloyd George &#8220;possesses a flexible voice of light, clear, and pleasant quality. &#8230; When in a practical mood he gets along on D and E, but at other times he bridges a considerable interval.&#8221;</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-balfour.png" alt="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v27%201904/page/140/mode/2up" width="753" height="447" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-74779" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-balfour.png 753w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-balfour-600x356.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-24-words-and-music-balfour-300x178.png 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 753px) 100vw, 753px" /></p>
<p>Prime Minister Arthur Balfour&#8217;s voice &#8220;has that undefinable something in its timbre which one listens for in a first-class baritone&#8217;s singing. &#8230; The note on which he does most speaking is somewhere between D and E, but he frequently ranges the octave from G to G.&#8221;</p>
